WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.980
We have learned how charges move through circuits.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.120
Now we meet a material whose charge carriers we can deliberately change.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.280
In a semiconductor, electrons can be excited into a conduction band, leaving

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.640
holes in the valence band.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.806
A hole is a missing electron state that behaves like a mobile positive

00:00:18.806 --> 00:00:19.220
carrier;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.780
it is not a proton traveling through the solid.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.800
In a pure, intrinsic semiconductor, thermal generation creates electrons and

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.760
holes in pairs, so their concentrations are equal.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.920
Doping introduces a small controlled amount of suitable impurity.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.540
In silicon, a donor impurity can supply an extra conduction electron.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.600
Electrons become the majority carriers, giving n type material.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.460
An acceptor impurity produces holes as the majority carriers, giving p type

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:47.820
material.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.580
The other carrier is still present as a minority carrier.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.080
Here is the trap: n type does not mean the whole sample has a net negative

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:56.460
charge.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.320
The ionized donor charges balance the added mobile electrons.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.400
Likewise, p type is not an electrically positive chunk of material.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:06.700
Pause and test the wording.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.920
If someone says holes are the only carriers in p type material, is that

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:11.260
correct?

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.060
No.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.640
They are the majority, not the only carriers.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.420
Temperature also affects carrier concentration, so semiconductor conductivity

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.440
is not described by a fixed universal resistance.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.767
For our JEE questions, begin with the carrier picture and electrical

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:27.380
neutrality

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:27.768
before moving to a junction.

00:01:27.768 --> 00:01:33.188
Bring p type and n type regions together, and something happens even without a

00:01:33.188 --> 00:01:33.488
battery.

00:01:34.188 --> 00:01:37.388
Electrons diffuse from the electron rich n side toward the p side.

00:01:38.288 --> 00:01:39.928
Holes diffuse in the opposite direction.

00:01:40.688 --> 00:01:44.128
Near the junction, recombination removes mobile electrons and holes.

00:01:44.828 --> 00:01:49.040
This leaves a region depleted of mobile majority carriers, but not empty of

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:49.208
all

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:49.548
charge.

00:01:50.368 --> 00:01:54.728
Positive ionized donors remain on the n side, and negative ionized acceptors

00:01:54.728 --> 00:01:55.928
remain on the p side.

00:01:56.528 --> 00:02:00.248
These ions are part of the crystal and do not wander through the circuit like

00:02:00.248 --> 00:02:00.928
mobile carriers.

00:02:01.568 --> 00:02:05.852
Their electric field points from the positive n side ions toward the negative

00:02:05.852 --> 00:02:05.908
p

00:02:05.908 --> 00:02:06.508
side ions.

00:02:07.048 --> 00:02:11.268
That field opposes further majority carrier diffusion and establishes a

00:02:11.268 --> 00:02:11.948
potential barrier.

00:02:12.708 --> 00:02:17.208
At thermal equilibrium, diffusion and drift currents balance, giving zero net

00:02:17.208 --> 00:02:18.788
current through the unbiased junction.

00:02:19.488 --> 00:02:21.848
Do not call this unbiased state reverse bias.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:26.148
Reverse bias requires an externally applied voltage of the appropriate

00:02:26.148 --> 00:02:26.628
polarity.

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:30.348
Picture people crossing a doorway because one room is crowded.

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:34.688
Their movement builds an opposing influence until the net flow balances.

00:02:35.408 --> 00:02:38.688
The analogy helps with the direction, but the real barrier comes from

00:02:38.688 --> 00:02:40.608
electrostatics, not a physical wall.

00:02:41.448 --> 00:02:44.088
Think for a moment: is the depletion layer free of charge?

00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:45.068
No.

00:02:45.448 --> 00:02:48.688
It has fixed space charge and relatively few mobile carriers.

00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:52.968
Distinguish mobile carriers, fixed ions, and net current.

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:57.568
Those three ideas explain why the junction responds so differently to the two

00:02:57.568 --> 00:02:58.168
battery connections.

00:02:58.632 --> 00:03:04.032
Connect the p side toward a battery's positive terminal and the n side toward

00:03:04.032 --> 00:03:05.032
its negative terminal.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.452
That is forward bias.

00:03:08.412 --> 00:03:12.732
The applied voltage reduces the junction barrier and narrows the depletion

00:03:12.732 --> 00:03:15.692
region, allowing a rapidly increasing current.

00:03:16.952 --> 00:03:20.712
Reverse the connection, and the barrier grows while the depletion region

00:03:20.712 --> 00:03:21.132
widens.

00:03:22.272 --> 00:03:26.992
A small reverse current can still flow before breakdown; real reverse current

00:03:26.992 --> 00:03:31.212
is not exactly zero and can depend on temperature and the device.

00:03:32.152 --> 00:03:36.712
On an I versus V graph, forward current rises nonlinearly.

00:03:36.712 --> 00:03:41.632
In reverse bias it remains small over a range, then can become large in

00:03:41.632 --> 00:03:42.012
breakdown.

00:03:42.992 --> 00:03:46.952
Ordinary diodes must stay within their voltage, current, and power ratings.

00:03:47.392 --> 00:03:50.452
Now let's use an explicitly simplified circuit model.

00:03:51.792 --> 00:03:55.732
Assume a forward conducting silicon diode has a constant drop of zero point

00:03:55.732 --> 00:03:56.612
seven volts.

00:03:57.752 --> 00:04:00.732
A five volt supply drives it through a one kilo ohm resistor.

00:04:01.652 --> 00:04:05.552
The resistor gets five minus zero point seven, or four point three volts.

00:04:06.432 --> 00:04:10.192
Divide by one thousand ohms: current is four point three milliamperes.

00:04:11.012 --> 00:04:15.252
The resistor limits current; connecting a diode directly to an ideal voltage

00:04:15.252 --> 00:04:17.632
source can be a poor model of a safe circuit.

00:04:18.532 --> 00:04:22.812
The zero point seven value is not a sharp universal physical threshold, and it

00:04:22.812 --> 00:04:24.492
changes with current and temperature.

00:04:25.792 --> 00:04:28.732
For JEE, first read the model specified in the question.

00:04:29.472 --> 00:04:33.852
An ideal diode has zero forward drop, whereas this constant drop approximation

00:04:33.852 --> 00:04:34.772
does not.

00:04:35.136 --> 00:04:37.956
An alternating source changes polarity.

00:04:38.456 --> 00:04:42.836
A diode can turn that alternating signal into a load current that does not

00:04:42.836 --> 00:04:43.676
reverse direction.

00:04:44.476 --> 00:04:49.164
In a simple half wave rectifier, a diode and resistor are in series with the

00:04:49.164 --> 00:04:49.596
source.

00:04:50.476 --> 00:04:53.376
Choose the orientation that conducts on the positive half cycle.

00:04:54.396 --> 00:04:59.036
During that half cycle the ideal diode acts like a short circuit, so the load

00:04:59.036 --> 00:05:00.416
follows the positive input.

00:05:01.236 --> 00:05:05.856
During the negative half cycle it blocks, and the ideal load voltage is zero.

00:05:06.576 --> 00:05:08.696
The result is a sequence of positive pulses.

00:05:09.596 --> 00:05:13.936
It has a nonzero average, but it is not smooth, constant direct voltage.

00:05:14.916 --> 00:05:19.896
For a sinusoidal input with peak V m, the ideal average over a complete cycle

00:05:19.896 --> 00:05:22.096
is V m divided by pi.

00:05:22.876 --> 00:05:26.296
The root mean square output is V m divided by two.

00:05:27.136 --> 00:05:31.356
These formulas assume an ideal diode, a resistive load, and no smoothing

00:05:31.356 --> 00:05:31.816
capacitor.

00:05:31.816 --> 00:05:37.066
For a ten volt peak input, the average is about three point one eight volts

00:05:37.066 --> 00:05:37.276
and

00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:39.196
the root mean square value is five volts.

00:05:40.176 --> 00:05:41.216
Those are different quantities.

00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:42.196
Pause before answering a frequency question.

00:05:42.196 --> 00:05:46.796
A fifty hertz input makes fifty positive output pulses per second in this half

00:05:46.796 --> 00:05:48.836
wave circuit, not a hundred.

00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:52.976
If the diode is reversed, the passed pulses have the opposite sign under the

00:05:52.976 --> 00:05:53.996
same voltage reference.

00:05:54.836 --> 00:05:57.776
Real diode drop and source resistance change the waveform.

00:05:58.716 --> 00:06:02.176
Keep the circuit orientation and the stated approximation attached to the

00:06:02.176 --> 00:06:02.376
formula.

00:06:02.688 --> 00:06:05.408
Can we use the energy in the other half cycle too?

00:06:05.988 --> 00:06:09.448
A full wave rectifier does that by redirecting current so the load current

00:06:09.448 --> 00:06:10.428
keeps the same direction.

00:06:10.808 --> 00:06:12.088
In a bridge there are four diodes.

00:06:12.648 --> 00:06:16.090
One pair conducts during one half cycle, and the other pair conducts during

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:16.228
the

00:06:16.228 --> 00:06:17.048
opposite half cycle.

00:06:18.088 --> 00:06:22.028
Trace a complete path from the currently positive source terminal, through a

00:06:22.028 --> 00:06:25.068
conducting diode, through the load, and back through the other conducting

00:06:25.068 --> 00:06:25.408
diode.

00:06:26.108 --> 00:06:28.008
Do not assume all four conduct at once.

00:06:28.528 --> 00:06:32.968
For ideal diodes and a resistive load, the output is the absolute value of the

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:33.828
sinusoidal input.

00:06:34.568 --> 00:06:37.088
Its average is twice V m divided by pi.

00:06:38.008 --> 00:06:41.388
With a ten volt peak input, that is about six point three seven volts.

00:06:42.128 --> 00:06:46.468
A fifty hertz input now gives one hundred output pulses each second, so ripple

00:06:46.468 --> 00:06:48.248
frequency is twice the source frequency.

00:06:48.948 --> 00:06:52.382
Under a constant zero point seven volt drop model, two conducting bridge

00:06:52.382 --> 00:06:52.668
diodes

00:06:52.668 --> 00:06:56.822
reduce the output peak to approximately eight point six volts for that same

00:06:56.822 --> 00:06:56.988
ten

00:06:56.988 --> 00:06:57.648
volt peak source.

00:06:58.098 --> 00:07:02.248
Do not use the ideal average formula unchanged for the entire real waveform.

00:07:03.248 --> 00:07:06.988
A capacitor across the load can charge near the peaks and discharge between

00:07:06.988 --> 00:07:08.088
them, reducing ripple.

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:12.333
It does not guarantee perfectly constant output, and the result depends on

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:12.528
load

00:07:12.528 --> 00:07:13.328
and capacitance.

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:16.248
Our graphs show the unsmoothed ideal case.

00:07:16.728 --> 00:07:20.988
For JEE, separate peak, average, root mean square, and ripple frequency, then

00:07:20.988 --> 00:07:23.348
count the conducting diode drops in the chosen circuit.
